Subject: [#94-1-567] SEMANTICS:  Faith

Stuart W. Card challenges:
>And now for my heresy: since reason is not ultimately
>'true' but merely useful (very much so and often),
>how about admitting the usefulness of other paths
>to knowledge, like MYSTICISM?

Mysticism could possibly be useful.  If a form of mysticism is shown to be 
repeatable, consistent, and reliable, I would gladly believe in it.  I 
agree with Suart's general point that rationalism can be a religeon, and a 
disbelief in mysticism can be a dogma.

Until we understand the entire universe through rationalism, I find some 
mysticism helpful.  Predicting emotional reactions, for example.  I can 
predict more than 50% of people's emotional response using logic.  I may 
get a better predictive result by imagining myself in the same position and 
wondering how I would feel.  I have no rational explanation for my 
conclusions, except that I am drawing on the same psychological unknown of 
the human brain as the othe person, and my results are sometimes going to 
be similar.

Art is another example.  I don't know much about art, but I know what I 
like.  I can predict what others will like and dislike more accurately by 
my own instinctive reaction to art than by a scientific analysis.  If I 
knew more about art, the odds might be different, but for right now I trust 
my instincts and subconscious more than my logical deductions about what is 
good art.

I know some people will probably claim that what I am doing is not 
mystical, but it fits my loose definition.  I somehow derive the answers I 
seek through some internal/subconscious method that I know not of.  Even if 
I can give a rational explaination for why this method should work, we 
still don't understand how it works.  I trust more in my subconscious 
because of my personal experience in it than any scientific rational.  This 
is probably the closest to a predictable, rational version of mysticism, 
magic, or ESP that I is generally understood today.  More esoteric versions 
of mysticism may likewise be discovered in some long, lost sect of secret 
societies.
__
