From: more@chaph.usc.edu (Max More)
Date: Wed, 26 Jan 1994 19:51:56 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-1-568] Principles:  Faith; Evolutionary ascendence

Me:
>> Faith in a better future is confidence that an external force, 
>> whether God, State, or extraterrestrials, will solve our problems.

Mike Price:
>This notion that faith is somehow irrational is discussed (and trounced)
>in _Atheism: the case against God_ by George(?) Smith.  [I may have the
>title and author slightly wrong, but Max should remember it - he lent
>it to me :-) ]  In it faith was discussed and shown to be like the web
>of trust envisaged in crypto-key rings for the distribution of public
>keys.  I have faith that the sun will rise tomorrow not because I have
>personally made observations and calculated its path (relative to the
>Earth) but because I am confident that lots of people more competent
>than me, with more time to spare, have done the task for me.  We all
>form judgements about the reliability of people in some areas (based on
>our own knowledge) and are prepared to accept that they probably 
>know what they're talking about in other areas of which we know less.

Mike, if you look again at the book (Atheism: The Case Against God by 
George H. Smith) you'll find that you're mistaken.  Especially review 
Ch.4, Reason Vs. Faith, and Ch.6, The Varieties of Faith.  Smith clearly 
opposes reason to faith, presenting the Christian idea of faith as belief in 
something in the absence of or despite the evidence.  In Ch.6, Smith 
examines the view of faith as trust in an authority (because one Christian 
defence against charges of irrationality is to assert that atheists also have 
faith, in the sense of "faith" in the utterances of experts and authorities).  
Smith grants that the word "faith" is used not only in the sense of blind 
irrationality but also in the sense of reasonable (and defeasible) trust in 
authorities (defeasible because rational trust in authority is always open 
to withdrawal and refutation).  However he says "We are concerned 
strictly with the concept of "faith" in a philosophical sense, i.e., as an 
alleged _alternative_ to reason."  Nowhere (that I can see) does Smith 
show "faith" to be like a web of trust.

>The entire modern scientific process is based on faith.  I have
>_critical_ faith in stuff I read in refereed journals.  A "good" journal
>establishes a reputation in the scientific market-place by carefully
>validating articles with reputable reviewers and attracting good quality
>work.  Scientists build up their reputations by publishing in respected
>journals.  The loop is closed.  The whole process relies on the rational
>use of faith.

>>  Faith, or the Pollyanna/Dr. Pangloss variety of optimism, breeds
>> passivity by promising progress as a gift bestowed on us by superior
>> forces.  But, in return for the gift, faith requires a fixed belief
>> in and supplication to external forces, thereby creating dogmatic
>> beliefs and irrationally rigid behavior.

>Again, that's not faith you're talking about, but _uncritical_ faith. 
>I have faith in lots of things.  So do you.

Do not!  :)   As Steve Whitrow helpfully pointed out, you are using "faith" 
in the sense of trust (or perhaps are not separating the idea of trust from 
that of irrational commitment), while I am using it in the sense of 
irrational belief without evidence or contrary to evidence. I think a better 
way of putting my view is that faith is belief in something in such a way 
as not to expose the believe to refutation.  This _is_ different because you 
might hold some believe in the absence of evidence, but immediately 
drop that belief as soon as disconfirming evidence comes along.  
According to Pancritical rationalism, your belief was not irrational before 
the evidence turned up, but becomes irrational only if it is resistant to the 
evidence.  On the traditional justificationist view, there is no difference 
between these two formulations, since a belief is irrational unless it is 
justified.

While your broader use of "faith" is reasonable (it is common usage), I do 
think it leads to confusion.  Regardless of that, in the Principles it seems 
tome to be clear that I am using the word such that faith is irrational.  As 
Steve noted, I sometimes attach the word "blind" to "faith" -- as in Self-
Transformation, 1st paragraph:  "We affirm a philosophy of life but 
distance ourselves from religious dogma becauseof its blind faith, 
debasement of human worth, and systematic irrationality.").  Why use the 
word "faith" confusingly in both senses, when we can use the perfectly 
good word "trust"?

Mike:
>> Extropians recognize the unique place of our species, and our
>> opportunity to advance nature's evolution to new peaks. Beginning as
>> mindless matter, parts of nature developed in a slow evolutionary
>> ascendence, leading to progressively more powerful brains.  

>This view of evolution that says we are the result of a slow but 
>certain ascendance is questionable.  Other interpretations say that we
>(intelligent, technological life) are a freak accident.    

>All this is not overwhelmingly convincing, but it does throw doubt on
>the popular view that evolution is a steady but slow progression from
>small brains to larger.  It is just as likely that we are accidents,
>with long plateaus in our evolution broken, perhaps, by improbable
>transitions. 

Mike, you seem to be misinterpreting what I wrote.  I referred to "a slow 
evolutionary ascendence"; you immediately refer to that by restating it as 
"a slow but certain ascendence".  Nothing in what I write states or implies 
any certainty to the evolution of larger brains from smaller.  I take no 
position on that question, and have no problem with your statement that 
"it is just as likely that we are accidents".  Again, you paraphrase me as 
talking about "steady but slow" then present the alternative of a more 
desultory evolution.  I did not use the word "steady".  I was painting a 
picture of an overall evolutionary process that Extropians want to 
continue and accelerate.  I did not say, and have no need to say, that this 
process has been either steady or certain.

Nonetheless, I appreciate the feedback on the Principles and hope to see 
more.  I already expect to add an even clearer and more unmissable 
"Think For Yourself" statement (though I think such an attitude is clearly 
expressed in the principles of Self-Transformation).

Upward and Outward!

