From: mlinksva@netcom.com (Michael R Linksvayer)
Date: Wed, 26 Jan 1994 22:04:38 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-1-572] Fractal compression and Internet commerce

Stuart Card writes:
> Mike Linksvayer suggests a need for a market for distributed
> computation.  I have given this a fair amount of thought over
> the last few months.  In a few more months, I will be setting
> up a rather large virtual subnetwork of the Internet.  Since
> my on-line archival storage requirements will be very high,
> I plan to offer my subscribers a route to offset their
> subscription costs: provide me with randomly hashed storage.

Can it really be cost effective to buy storage from people over the net
vs. buying more local disks? Computation seems much more appropriate
for a distributed system: I'd guess that most potential computations
are wasted, while not all that much storage space goes unused, and it
is simple for a computation-seller to give tasks higher or lower
priority based on his needs at the moment, wheras there are only a few
possible priority levels for storage (in RAM, on disk, compressed on
disk, and offline).

In any case, good luck, sounds interesting.

Another possible implication for distributed computation markets: a
massive increase in compute-power, even without improvements in chip
technologies. If a fair percentage of the processing power available on
the Internet (or to be available in the next few years) could be
efficiently harnessed with distributed computation markets, rather than
mostly wasted, as I suspect it is now, some large problems that require
massive amounts of processing could be tackled, as long as they can be
distributed effectively.

A few have commented that many AI techniques simply require more
processing power than is currently available to be used effectively.
Could any of these be distributed? (wild, uninformed speculation
follows) If so, would it be possible to create a recognizably
intelligent entity, but with a very slow response time, in say ten
years after computation markets take off? (Remember the Mailman in
Vinge's _True Names_)

