>(Sorry, I couldn't resist!)

OOH!  I like that one!  I'm glad you succumbed to the temptation.

Just to make this more than a wasteful "me too!" message, I'll post
a couple of the problems I see with pancritical rationalism:

1) As Ray as already pointed out, it seems to suffer from what academic
philosophers call "self-referential refutation," or what Branden called the
fallacy of self-exclusion.  It seems to fail to address the question of why
it should be considered "credible," on its own terms.

2)  It seems to merely replace "true or false" with "credible or incredible,"
a substitution which seems to neither improve upon things or escape the alleged
problems of what it's supposed to replace.  It's all very well and good to
allow for some propositions to be taken more seriously than others, even within
the class of serious ones, but it doesn't seem to make any difference whether
you say that you're more certain of some truths than others or some are more
credible than others.

3) It seems to assume that minds are random-idea generators, failing to address
the question of the origins of beliefs/awareness/concepts, merely assuming
that they do.  If this isn't contrary to your experience (it is to mine), then
I'd be surprised.

Needless to say, I'm looking forward to Max's talk on this subject at Extro1,
and hope he'll address some of these questions.

